BODY = '''<p>A tub transfer bench lets you sit down outside the tub and slide in, so you lift your legs over the tub wall while seated instead of stepping over it standing. It is a simple piece of equipment, but the setup and the order of movements matter. This page gathers the steps from three public sources: a rehabilitation handout from the Greater Baltimore Center for Rehabilitation Medicine, the Canadian Red Cross instructions for its bath transfer bench, and the Health New Zealand home equipment page. Not sure a bench is the right seat? Read our comparison of a <a href="/articles/shower-chair-or-transfer-bench/">shower chair and a transfer bench</a> first.</p>
<p class="note"><strong>These are general steps, not a prescription.</strong> The Red Cross calls its instructions "guidelines only" and says to use the bench only as your healthcare provider instructs. A doctor or occupational therapist can watch you transfer and adjust the steps for your body.</p>

<h2>Set Up the Bench First</h2>
<p>The Red Cross instructions start with the legs. Most tub floors sit higher than the bathroom floor, so the legs inside the tub and the legs outside it need different heights. Set the two inside legs to the same height and the two outside legs to the same height, so the bench ends up approximately level. The legs must reach the floor, and the bench should not rest on the tub wall.</p>
<p>Health New Zealand gives the same rule in plainer words: set the legs at a height for the person using the bench and so it is level. On both pages, you adjust a leg by pushing in its metal button and sliding the leg to the next hole. The button must then pop all the way out through the hole. The Red Cross puts this in a warning, and Health New Zealand says to check that the buttons are completely popped out.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Set the heights.</strong> Inside legs together, outside legs together, bench roughly level.</li>
<li><strong>Check each button.</strong> Every spring button should protrude fully through its hole.</li>
<li><strong>Place the bench over the tub wall.</strong> Two legs go inside the tub, as close to the far wall as possible. The Red Cross says the handle should be on the same side as the wall, and to call the Red Cross for guidance if it is not.</li>
<li><strong>Check the feet.</strong> If your bench has suction cups, confirm they are holding the bench firmly in place, as Health New Zealand advises. Drive Medical sells suction cups for its 12011KD-1 bench separately, so a bench you buy may not come with them.</li>
<li><strong>Test it.</strong> The Red Cross says to always test the bench for stability before use.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Getting In From Standing</h2>
<p>The Greater Baltimore Center for Rehabilitation Medicine handout shows four moves. The Red Cross and Health New Zealand describe the same sequence in more detail.</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Back up to the bench</strong> until you can feel it against your legs.</li>
<li><strong>Reach back and lower yourself onto the seat.</strong> The Red Cross says to sit on the side of the bench that is outside the tub. The handout says to scoot back as far as you can.</li>
<li><strong>Turn toward the tub and lift your legs over the wall.</strong> The handout says to scoot further onto the seat as you go. The Red Cross says to slide to the center of the bench and lift each leg over the tub wall, holding the side arm if your bench has one. Health New Zealand says to use the handle if you need it.</li>
<li><strong>Settle in the center of the bench,</strong> then wash.</li>
</ol>
<p>The Red Cross also says to turn slowly and without twisting, to avoid injury.</p>

<h2>Getting Out</h2>
<p>The rehab handout says to reverse the steps. Health New Zealand says the same for both a bath and a shower. In the Red Cross wording, slide to the edge of the bench after bathing and move your legs out of the tub, turn slowly, then press down on the seat and stand. MedlinePlus recommends a grab bar to help you get in and out of the tub. See where to place one in <a href="/articles/where-to-put-grab-bars/">our grab bar placement article</a>.</p>

<h2>If You Move From a Wheelchair</h2>
<p>The Red Cross has a separate sequence for a wheelchair user:</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li>Place the wheelchair next to the bench, facing the front of the bathtub, and engage the wheel locks.</li>
<li>Move your torso onto the bench first, then lift your legs into the tub, turning slowly without twisting.</li>
<li>Slide to a comfortable position in the center of the tub.</li>
<li>After bathing, lift your legs out of the tub first, then lower your torso into the wheelchair.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Checks Before Every Shower</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Level and locked.</strong> The Red Cross says to always test the bench for stability before use, and to check that the spring buttons on the legs protrude fully.</li>
<li><strong>A mat in the tub.</strong> The Red Cross advises a slip-resistant bath mat when using the bench. MedlinePlus likewise suggests non-slip suction mats or rubber silicone decals in the bottom of the tub and a non-skid mat outside it.</li>
<li><strong>Weight capacity.</strong> Health New Zealand notes that most transfer benches have a maximum weight capacity and gives no figure. Drive Medical lists 400 pounds for its 12011KD-1 bench on its own page. Check the label on the one you own.</li>
<li><strong>Someone nearby.</strong> The Red Cross recommends supervision or assistance for users with limited mobility.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Bench in a Tub Versus a Shower</h2>
<p>Health New Zealand describes both. In a shower, the order is the same: sit on the end of the bench, slide along it until you are in the shower, and turn your bottom around so your legs go in. It says to make sure there is enough room to get your legs into the shower while you sit on the bench, and that you can take the backrest off if you do not need it. It places the bench with the handle and suction feet inside the bath or shower.</p>

<h2>Will It Fit My Tub?</h2>
<p>None of the three handouts gives tub measurements, and Drive Medical's page for its 12011KD-1 bench lists the seat at 26 inches wide and 18.5 to 19.5 inches deep and calls the bench reversible "to accommodate any bathroom," with no range of tub widths. Before you buy, compare the width and depth on the maker's page with your own tub and with the floor space beside it, where the outside legs will stand. The seat height on that bench runs from 17.5 to 22.5 inches in half-inch steps. A therapist can tell you the height that suits you.</p>

<h2>Caring for the Bench</h2>
<p>The Red Cross says to wipe the bench dry after every use and wash it with mild soapy water, avoiding abrasive cleaners.</p>

<h2>Transfer Bench Questions</h2>
<h3>Do I need someone with me?</h3>
<p>The Red Cross recommends supervision or assistance for users with limited mobility. Health New Zealand says your occupational therapist should choose a bench suited to you.</p>
<h3>What if the bench rocks?</h3>
<p>Re-check the leg heights and the buttons before you sit. The Red Cross says the bench should be approximately level with its legs reaching the floor, and it should not rest on the tub wall.</p>
<h3>Which way should the handle face?</h3>
<p>The Red Cross says the handle should be on the same side as the wall. If it is not, the Red Cross tells its borrowers to call for guidance. Drive Medical says its 12011KD-1 bench is reversible.</p>
<h3>Does a bench replace grab bars?</h3>
<p>No. MedlinePlus recommends two grab bars, one to help you in and out of the tub and one to help you stand, fixed to the wall vertically or horizontally and not diagonally.</p>
<h3>Will insurance pay for one?</h3>
<p>It depends on the plan. See <a href="/articles/does-medicare-pay-for-mobility-equipment/">our page on what Medicare pays for</a>.</p>

<h2>Choosing a Bench</h2>
<p>Our <a href="/best-shower-chairs/">shower chair and transfer bench guide</a> compares a Drive Medical tub transfer bench with four shower chairs and stools, with the weight limits and seat sizes from the makers' pages.</p>'''

